 "-\\frac{y+\\frac{5}{2}}{x+\\frac{7}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545122} {:answer/id #uuid "45cf24a6-252c-576a-a3c7-260cfefc5131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x+\\frac{7}{2}}{2(y+\\frac{5}{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545129} {:answer/id #uuid "7e04e4bd-66eb-5c88-81ec-42881430b73f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x+\\frac{7}{2}}{y+\\frac{5}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545144} {:answer/id #uuid "c35bfeb4-9909-5b13-855b-2f77080d2544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+\\frac{7}{2}}{y+\\frac{5}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545168} {:answer/id #uuid "c5de0c5f-2f81-5365-8771-6e4dfebb0d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2(x+\\frac{7}{2})}{y+\\frac{5}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e04e4bd-66eb-5c88-81ec-42881430b73f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x+\\frac{7}{2}}{y+\\frac{5}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545144} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8496e57-39dd-5007-8692-dfaaa090151c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d3121025-6611-531b-af37-770f4473c17e" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5b008917c1a1bc0f2109fd614d9470b4c805bb09dcad15dc50dafd7dc9b177ed.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the circle shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our circle is centered at $(- \\frac{7}{2},- \\frac{5}{2})$ and has a radius of $\\frac{3}{2},$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-(- \\frac{7}{2}))^{2}+(y-(- \\frac{5}{2}))^{2}={\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}\\,⟹\\,(x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{5}{2})^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{5}{2})^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((y+\\frac{5}{2})^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+\\frac{7}{2})+2(y+\\frac{5}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2(y+\\frac{5}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2(x+\\frac{7}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+\\frac{7}{2}}{y+\\frac{5}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085d5f70-a758-5ee6-a237-cd70c5daefa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x+\\frac{7}{2}}{y+\\frac{3}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545114} {:answer/id #uuid "38581ad1-e00c-5166-b6b8-da5f0f1b1c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{y+\\frac{3}{2}}{x+\\frac{7}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545128} {:answer/id #uuid "956a17cd-d604-5800-ba68-8c52c52b2d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x+\\frac{7}{2}}{2(y+\\frac{3}{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545150} {:answer/id #uuid "a1cba8f8-c370-5d2d-bf85-e6448daf4ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+\\frac{7}{2}}{y+\\frac{3}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545155} {:answer/id #uuid "ab29b439-c053-5ab4-b58f-3a6dcca32183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2(x+\\frac{7}{2})}{y+\\frac{3}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "085d5f70-a758-5ee6-a237-cd70c5daefa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x+\\frac{7}{2}}{y+\\frac{3}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545114} :answer-field/id #uuid "516481b8-d8c8-58ca-bc4f-15b8b0a2b0f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e666840a-1052-52e3-badf-2168db98672a" :question/problem "![](images/02/025ef38d69954bfcc7f2a463a97239e442c446803ede7fee6ee172b30a356cbd.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the circle shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our circle is centered at $(- \\frac{7}{2},- \\frac{3}{2})$ and has a radius of $\\frac{3}{2},$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-(- \\frac{7}{2}))^{2}+(y-(- \\frac{3}{2}))^{2}={\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}\\,⟹\\,(x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{3}{2})^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{3}{2})^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((y+\\frac{3}{2})^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+\\frac{7}{2})+2(y+\\frac{3}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2(y+\\frac{3}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2(x+\\frac{7}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+\\frac{7}{2}}{y+\\frac{3}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aa037eb-acb4-59c7-9bc8-b5a91aeab6ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-6}^{-1}({[10-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545289} {:answer/id #uuid "73772f39-b6a2-53e5-a227-0d97808f09d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{6}({[10-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545417} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9e7748-eb16-59b5-abdd-e671cdbf6651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}({[7-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{6}{y}]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545488} {:answer/id #uuid "d032dc32-0433-5a8b-bab4-f8aeb0c6d4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}({[10-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545577} {:answer/id #uuid "ebffd986-5bc8-51e2-b270-81cdea5769e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}({[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d032dc32-0433-5a8b-bab4-f8aeb0c6d4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}({[10-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545577} :answer-field/id #uuid "c103ad26-447b-5ac7-aeb2-5c94016982cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08b6d6f3-0fab-52e6-98e5-6de56edc9807" :question/math-academy-id "q-50331" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d930c097ed0ddfa454d7f443112c2d9e233e29521e8950fcc4738e742dd3d8dc.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{6}{y}$ and $x=7-y$ for $y\\in [1,6]$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=-3.$  Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/ed/edea788893771f13f3afc7d9f49e8f1dead4e9b589ca78ef160b9bddccf867c5.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\frac{6}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=7-y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=7-y.$\n\n![](images/48/48df22796616a48f9e9ed56474ced12392905e976fdec064b401894f30cbf49b.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}[\\,(7-y)-(-3)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}{[10-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\frac{6}{y}.$\n\n![](images/f3/f3240bb3d94231ae5b786b01b2fe11f9e0ea7971978e54f26a4e36b84af71a28.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}{[\\,(\\frac{6}{y})-(-3)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}{[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}{[10-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}{[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}({[10-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{6}{y}+3]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545799 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bddb17aa-bb31-5167-aec7-5f150d00bbdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bddb17aa-bb31-5167-aec7-5f150d00bbdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545546} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cafba33-0210-5ea7-a371-41e466aabf80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545702} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "951aa75b-480f-5a6a-85e6-78d9055600b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "951aa75b-480f-5a6a-85e6-78d9055600b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545470} :answer-field/id #uuid "c14e8247-0744-5637-b0b4-5ca12a64d825" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d83a7e0-a78a-57e3-b959-267d62524fd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-324152" :question/problem "![](images/59/59ad56e7eae8e8598ebd511a7b499f35cd6a178405f8198831b23e8d5d47a513.png)\n\nThe circles $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $  intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of the point $A?$ Express the polar angle $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ in radians.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we notice from the graph that the origin $O$ is an obvious first point of intersection. \n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n \n\n$$\n(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d0c35fb-e6e8-5a53-a69f-4b99e754b3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{6}({[6-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{10}{y}-1]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545293} {:answer/id #uuid "637dca43-26dc-54b2-8acd-b44b28e36455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}({[6-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{10}{y}-1]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545387} {:answer/id #uuid "66837ddd-4434-54af-8776-16b6edb795ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}({[y-7]}^{2}-{[\\frac{10}{y}]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545395} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c46529-231a-5bc3-89ad-ebb9317619d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5}({[6-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{10}{y}-1]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545626} {:answer/id #uuid "f97511ec-a987-529f-a0fa-f202424d84c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}({[\\frac{10}{y}-1]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "637dca43-26dc-54b2-8acd-b44b28e36455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}({[6-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{10}{y}-1]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545387} :answer-field/id #uuid "44a1a3e4-d370-5a47-b2e6-255c12ad4fca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "423623d2-e3d9-5c6f-a470-2f2a329c961b" :question/math-academy-id "q-50428" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5ea4fcb15db81b35a8a6e399cc3df734fc1dd41afe42799b8ca2a39734996cb.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=-\\frac{10}{y}$ and $x=y-7$ for $y\\in [2,5]$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=-1.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/98/987999e84c2b22c8b90ecb0381518e7504d0b792ecebb067a7221653422b3e89.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=-\\frac{10}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=y-7.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=y-7.$\n\n![](images/f8/f8c250c5fbf20980050a1e86f4ac5c91bff7b24bfc7ce5394f33c87703139026.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}[\\,(-1)-(y-7)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}{[6-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=-\\frac{10}{y}.$\n\n![](images/64/64b440be918c616eb07f05f7f09cb8e14ed649f677afc5f40f66f47ec34ed87e.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}{[\\,(-1)-(-\\frac{10}{y})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}{[\\frac{10}{y}-1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}{[6-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}{[\\frac{10}{y}-1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}({[6-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{10}{y}-1]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545819 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "137571b5-7a83-5f63-8a0b-fcf213a92f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545235} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff39d9b-b653-56bf-aa12-7a8401ca193a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545348} {:answer/id #uuid "b258fa98-837b-58ac-bb50-87dbe84ce48b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545521} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f2c0d5-f400-5694-82c6-4b8a8fb68adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545529} {:answer/id #uuid "da283058-2c21-59b1-a080-2b672e4fb121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4f2c0d5-f400-5694-82c6-4b8a8fb68adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545529} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f86bd3-1e4e-5923-9c4e-69ac157875db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f375dc2-c494-589d-83fe-86d9688ddf86" :question/math-academy-id "q-54386" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians to an equivalent measure in degrees. This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\pi }{6})(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{6}={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we know that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "107da7cc-1b85-5247-b8b7-0e025ec0a5af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{153\\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "107da7cc-1b85-5247-b8b7-0e025ec0a5af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{153\\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545228} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6b62faf-4750-56ae-8c93-f35beee1fd2d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6932dce0-68da-5dc2-a092-7a3088f2337d" :question/math-academy-id "q-361838" :question/problem "![](images/ce/ce9f6280200d6f71fc13b4dba705c26341ddf11d369b547e05c8647c0e1c3e8e.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x={y}^{2}-2$ and $x=y$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=3.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, to determine the limits of integration, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2 & =y \\\\ {y}^{2}-y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)(y+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=2.$\n\nNext, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/74/74e298b85aa9e300d303fa34c761d5ffad214110e315ccf18c33edd2283210ad.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x={y}^{2}-2.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x={y}^{2}-2.$\n\n![](images/94/945dfc9ade4dc95a336f6c0111ea50496242ca60dfddff61f85961547c0c7d3e.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[\\,(3)-({y}^{2}-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[5-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y.$\n\n![](images/9e/9e25b8fd45f666927edbfec7d9ccd714d14037784f86fb9a8493bf861d521ff9.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[\\,(3)-(y)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[3-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[5-{y}^{2}]}^{2}-{[3-y]}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}((25-10{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})-(9-6y+{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({y}^{4}-11{y}^{2}+6y+16)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{11{y}^{3}}{3}+3{y}^{2}+16y)|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{11({2}^{3})}{3}+3({2}^{2})+16(2))-(-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{11}{3}+3-16)] \\\\  & =\\pi [\\frac{316}{15}-(-\\frac{143}{15})] \\\\  & =\\frac{153\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd45d89c-18f2-5af8-af3a-72cc7f53e8f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd45d89c-18f2-5af8-af3a-72cc7f53e8f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545574} :answer-field/id #uuid "1789f9e8-fa16-5937-94c1-1a6d7f4887e0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545683} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54e10cfb-4665-5024-a9c4-1624bf7688ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54e10cfb-4665-5024-a9c4-1624bf7688ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545358} :answer-field/id #uuid "417ba2b3-92db-5f71-9eb2-99d78f38ddf7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a30f6995-a7bb-57e9-9de6-99d42c4d49a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-324194" :question/problem "The curves $r=2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ intersect at a single point in the third quadrant. Determine the polar coordinates of this point. Express the polar angle $\\theta $ in radians.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2 & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the $\\theta $-coordinate of the point that we're interested in is $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$ since it lies in the third quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of our point:\n\n$$\nr=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the desired intersection point are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{7}{2},\\text{ }\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545851 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5748d238-2d62-5c05-9b32-cc45a9cb23cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545363} {:answer/id #uuid "7a34109a-a5f7-5e0d-9dea-2408d0c9fe14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545430} {:answer/id #uuid "9b661e8b-5a92-5a04-a768-7616735c352b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545479} {:answer/id #uuid "c4277d9a-1776-504e-a740-20236a450250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545554} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e6e99c-04e1-5531-ac7d-3bc194756e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4277d9a-1776-504e-a740-20236a450250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545554} :answer-field/id #uuid "00c32dba-43b3-51e4-90b4-f83a74a787dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b30c011d-e3fe-5a6f-9c15-6828aaa923bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-121848" :question/problem "The curves $r=2-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ intersect at a single point in the second quadrant. Determine the polar coordinates of this point." :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the $\\theta $-coordinate of the point that we're interested in is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ since it lies in the second quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of our point:\n\n$$\nr=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the desired intersection point are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a11dbc5-4605-5d2f-815d-487cdff0ae65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545252} {:answer/id #uuid "4a933bd3-9d4d-5635-b3d2-b0ca7cff973e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545341} {:answer/id #uuid "b56b841a-b4a3-56c7-bfa1-a682e8e14500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545530} {:answer/id #uuid "bb5dd636-0a57-50f3-a3b6-1ac51a1c9db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545538} {:answer/id #uuid "bd197a7f-2045-5bf2-9e98-404a33fb8ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb5dd636-0a57-50f3-a3b6-1ac51a1c9db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545538} :answer-field/id #uuid "27912ff0-a6bc-5a3b-9141-25760180fb99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c68e45a3-bbd1-5372-b99a-a88344f8278f" :question/math-academy-id "q-54387" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ radians to an equivalent measure in degrees. This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\pi }{3})(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{3}={60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we know that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\sqrt{3},$  we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26e6c9f3-21eb-55ea-9e4f-d0db71448f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26e6c9f3-21eb-55ea-9e4f-d0db71448f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545285} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fd15637-13c3-5b02-a6e6-7167a6179c28" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545694} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6d5555a-8e5b-5383-bfa8-67d65b8ccbe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6d5555a-8e5b-5383-bfa8-67d65b8ccbe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545628} :answer-field/id #uuid "564db509-6137-5b97-b4e7-601a8c1e8728" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545710} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddbc8538-19fd-5aae-827d-018fd0e5ba24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddbc8538-19fd-5aae-827d-018fd0e5ba24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545608} :answer-field/id #uuid "90685216-2313-5ba6-972a-4dedde848c5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545741} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f429073a-dfbf-5e35-999b-44ef38ac203b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f429073a-dfbf-5e35-999b-44ef38ac203b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545649} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd614a72-9c5a-56ae-accf-fea2114cc61f" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c975a336-1c2a-5428-a315-180711c9bbb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-324172" :question/problem "![](images/ce/ce263ef057316e67e65b3adf1a5d66797c6a97c9bdcd33cae866d9111e523e10.png)\n\nThe curves $r=\\sqrt{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively? Express the polar angles ${\\theta }_{A}$ and ${\\theta }_{B}$ in radians.\n\n$({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$),\\,$\n\n$({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=\\sqrt{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinates of the intersection points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=-4\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {r}_{2} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=-4\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are\n\n$$\n({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3da82ce9-78a2-543b-b42d-6a1dff04ede5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{200}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545317} {:answer/id #uuid "73f2b6d9-290a-5c57-9e0d-31e098d34378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545418} {:answer/id #uuid "840b7aa1-f717-5655-85ec-596b4ab1d025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{200}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545448} {:answer/id #uuid "cf7dd604-9a13-5ed1-a0b7-6e46c2373cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545575} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e99de4-ede4-589b-af99-ad16705b718a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "840b7aa1-f717-5655-85ec-596b4ab1d025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{200}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545448} :answer-field/id #uuid "12a1b473-68e4-5c3b-8125-45d0cb822b91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb67f6a8-9365-5c2f-8040-e07ca796601e" :question/math-academy-id "q-146390" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{7}xy, & 0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{7}y(4-{y}^{2}), & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}]$ given that the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|\\frac{1}{2}}=\\frac{7}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{8}{7}xy}{\\frac{4}{7}y(4-{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{4-{y}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le 2.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=0.$\n\nNow, substituting $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the expression for ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2x}{(4-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})}=\\frac{2x}{(\\frac{15}{4})}=\\frac{8}{15}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{15}x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}{\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{1/2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[{x}^{4}]_{1/2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(16-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{255}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}]-{\\mu }_{X|\\frac{1}{2}}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{8}-{(\\frac{7}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{8}-\\frac{49}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{200}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1489df57-7fc5-588f-96d3-c120cf10a7d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3.0,{197.5}^{∘})$ and $(3.0,{342.5}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545237} {:answer/id #uuid "20e83cd9-7cc6-5c6f-ac11-378a2d41900d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3.4,{191.5}^{∘})$ and $(3.4,{348.5}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545265} {:answer/id #uuid "570d7b76-2255-5c9a-bbf6-0130f9b43c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3.1,{189.6}^{∘})$ and $(3.1,{350.4}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545362} {:answer/id #uuid "5dba6239-f541-5546-9927-92a968513a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3.5,{202.4}^{∘})$ and $(3.5,{337.6}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545378} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6e0900-8fbd-5105-8ac6-daf150f9d1cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3.2,{194.3}^{∘})$ and $(3.2,{345.7}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20e83cd9-7cc6-5c6f-ac11-378a2d41900d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3.4,{191.5}^{∘})$ and $(3.4,{348.5}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545265} :answer-field/id #uuid "883b05a3-cd95-5802-ba49-1d8e57c33615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cdda3bdc-973b-5424-bda2-1798eb90cfd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-121827" :question/problem "![](images/ca/caabcee019c7e97355b6a30f820872207e3f43245d97d3d09a44bf400628a975.png)\n\nFind the polar coordinates of the intersection points of the polar curves $r=3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above. Round your answers to $1$ decimal place where appropriate." :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}≈{191.5}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}≈348.5,$ rounded to $1$ decimal place. \n\nNow, we use the equation $r=4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinates of the intersection points: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1} & =4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{1}=4+3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})=\\frac{17}{5}=3.4 \\\\ {r}_{2} & =4+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{2}=4+3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})=\\frac{17}{5}=3.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )≈(3.4,{191.5}^{∘})\\,$ and $\\,(r,\\theta )≈(3.4,{348.5}^{∘}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3704891e-617a-5863-9a03-baf25c99600b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545312} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4c20c4-9a93-56b4-8882-ad1c931d6d09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545315} {:answer/id #uuid "77363dd6-1e0e-5193-8140-a9949d0605e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545421} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d98834-f4f5-587e-83de-a18d83a26a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545615} {:answer/id #uuid "eed61002-51ff-5a1c-9ad4-f4d067a9f8d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1d98834-f4f5-587e-83de-a18d83a26a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545615} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d2588d2-34f5-552a-8d6d-c5e7c03f7737" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da13a004-2a50-58d1-9227-22e3b27eef81" :question/math-academy-id "q-149429" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,4)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3{y}^{11/3}}, & \\,1\\le y \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=4]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,4)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|4}=\\frac{8}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=4] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3{y}^{11/3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{3{y}^{5/3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{3}{y}^{-5/3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{8}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot {y}^{-2/3}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{4}{{y}^{2/3}}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =0-(-4) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=4] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=4]-{\\mu }_{Y|4}^{2} \\\\  & =4-{(\\frac{8}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{64}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b77ba13-51d9-5349-b156-c9977d72aec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b77ba13-51d9-5349-b156-c9977d72aec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545457} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed3bb5b2-9573-5648-9ac1-e2c7b129d109" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545783} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfbe5359-0657-5fe4-a2f2-94b550f4445f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfbe5359-0657-5fe4-a2f2-94b550f4445f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545613} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa646c97-4258-5a7b-a5e5-2be260e50b9a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eaf14619-5650-5c49-bb13-301ef3e6d48c" :question/math-academy-id "q-324164" :question/problem "![](images/51/515266ff58d998d660634860a07f26e65e0a2791a19a87e1a0f0cd8b8839c9b0.png)\n\nThe circles $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of the point $A?$ Express the polar angle $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ in radians.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we notice from the graph that the origin $O$ is an obvious first point of intersection. \n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n$$\n(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193ea9e3-bd8d-542f-b149-f78e0b396574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{252\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "193ea9e3-bd8d-542f-b149-f78e0b396574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{252\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545249} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ca58cc0-f38a-5004-9064-b0da06a85013" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f01f287c-3406-5e67-8ee1-f944f0c8187c" :question/math-academy-id "q-361835" :question/problem "![](images/35/353b6ad37a77d200719a155e8c14a974f01de00a2e4b4ca3312ce50401a715b7.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x={y}^{2}+2$ and $y=x-4$ for $y\\in [-1,2]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-2.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, since we are rotating about a vertical axis, we need to solve the equation of our second curve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-4\\,⟹\\,x=y+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7e/7e08a30f9ef1f8ce748ba0fcac73f1e93d994e4fd3a3d3d1367568c89c29f4d0.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x={y}^{2}+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=y+4.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=y+4.$\n\n![](images/26/262546bf3f1d1c9025d23b9fb134c9de7b5299c7b6383563531dffa62244163d.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[\\,(y+4)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[y+6]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x={y}^{2}+2.$\n\n![](images/ce/cefab13ca3d09fcf596e7906384c2efd1718c289783792f1b0ff950632c823ad.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[\\,({y}^{2}+2)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[{y}^{2}+4]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of our solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[y+6]}^{2}-{[{y}^{2}+4]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({y}^{2}+12y+36-({y}^{4}+8{y}^{2}+16))\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}(12y+20-{y}^{4}-7{y}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(6{y}^{2}+20y-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{7{y}^{3}}{3})|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi [\\,(6\\cdot {2}^{2}+20\\cdot 2-\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{7\\cdot {2}^{3}}{3})\\,-\\,(6-20+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{7}{3})\\,] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{584}{15}-(-\\frac{172}{15})) \\\\  & =\\frac{252\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545877 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "155ff5aa-4252-5ac2-80e1-ab36e6819d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{450}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545238} {:answer/id #uuid "63386fd4-c351-5fcf-a204-868e364ac71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{350}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545386} {:answer/id #uuid "9519ed18-f682-5e18-95a3-d2bb7a09c5cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545469} {:answer/id #uuid "bd742401-7fbe-51ac-b99e-fa8961304350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545544} {:answer/id #uuid "efcb4a86-108b-540b-8958-93dcf800b027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{450}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "155ff5aa-4252-5ac2-80e1-ab36e6819d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{450}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545238} :answer-field/id #uuid "08777c93-71f1-55d9-8b74-18999d083645" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa5bb889-1d66-5ec8-b499-bab2b60bc9f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-146830" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(x+2y)}{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+4y}{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=1]$ given that the corresponding expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|1}=\\frac{8}{15}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2(x+2y)}{3})}{(\\frac{1+4y}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2y)}{1+4y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=0.$ \n\nNow, substituting $y=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{2(x+2\\cdot 1)}{1+4\\cdot 1}=\\frac{2x+4}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(\\frac{2x+4}{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{4}{3})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\frac{11}{6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=1]-{\\mu }_{X|1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{30}-{(\\frac{8}{15})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{37}{450}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546039 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f3b1be-7698-59e3-aef0-38dd300bf844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- 12 e x + 6 + 3 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546026} {:answer/id #uuid "15a35f43-a4b7-584e-b180-d26a6e86a916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- 12 e \\left(x + \\frac{7}{4}\\right) + 3 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546028} {:answer/id #uuid "631a33cb-a6bf-5512-a0f9-90d28f159483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- 12 e x - 18 e + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546042} {:answer/id #uuid "a9fdf460-843a-56b3-b956-5712f66fb700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3 e \\left(x + \\frac{7}{4}\\right) + 6 + 3 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546055} {:answer/id #uuid "d18d0448-5ee2-554a-b226-5c667ed7dbe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=12 e \\left(x + \\frac{7}{4}\\right) + 6 + 3 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "631a33cb-a6bf-5512-a0f9-90d28f159483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- 12 e x - 18 e + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546042} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7fdb164-208e-53f2-a5fb-7d65ea9753b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "52a84e58-ee14-59cb-b2fc-5dc806f58a59" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=3{e}^{-4x-6}+6$ at the point where $x=- \\frac{7}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{u}+6,\\,u=-4x-6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=3{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{e}^{u}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-12{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-12{e}^{-4x-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=- \\frac{7}{4}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=- \\frac{7}{4}} & =-12{e}^{-4x-6}{|}_{x=- \\frac{7}{4}} \\\\ & =-12{e}^{(-4)(- \\frac{7}{4})-6} \\\\ & =- 12 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=x_1=- \\frac{7}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y_1 & =(3{e}^{-4x-6}+6){|}_{x=- \\frac{7}{4}} \\\\ & =3{e}^{(-4)(- \\frac{7}{4})-6}+6 \\\\ & =6 + 3 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(6 + 3 e) & =(- 12 e)(x-(- \\frac{7}{4})) \\\\ y-(6 + 3 e) & =- 12 e x-(21 e) \\\\ y & =- 12 e x - 18 e + 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00fd1e00-d8ad-53a1-a4ce-5e09d463daa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- 2 e \\left(x - \\frac{1}{3}\\right) - 2 e + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546022} {:answer/id #uuid "0d3a07ef-c7b6-5bda-8a5f-02f36078cba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6 e x - 4 e + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546027} {:answer/id #uuid "386252c4-f73b-5706-99ea-15795c9c3a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6 e \\left(x - \\frac{1}{3}\\right) - 2 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546031} {:answer/id #uuid "9237bc66-6da2-5b5f-af2c-8e5ce3b5744d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- 6 e \\left(x - \\frac{1}{3}\\right) - 2 e + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546049} {:answer/id #uuid "d0bc922d-bb0e-5a70-b6c9-97af108437ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6 e x - 2 e + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d3a07ef-c7b6-5bda-8a5f-02f36078cba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6 e x - 4 e + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546027} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0779349-01a1-5902-a3ac-f744fc1c935b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fd12a30-30af-5a77-a4c8-bf313d7546d2" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=-2{e}^{-3x+2}+3$ at the point where $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=-2{e}^{u}+3,\\,u=-3x+2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-2{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{u}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6{e}^{u} \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{1}{3}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=\\frac{1}{3}} & =6{e}^{-3x+2}{|}_{x=\\frac{1}{3}} \\\\ & =6{e}^{(-3)(\\frac{1}{3})+2} \\\\ & =6 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=x_1=\\frac{1}{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y_1 & =(-2{e}^{-3x+2}+3){|}_{x=\\frac{1}{3}} \\\\ & =-2{e}^{(-3)(\\frac{1}{3})+2}+3 \\\\ & =3 - 2 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(3 - 2 e) & =(6 e)(x-(\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\ y-(3 - 2 e) & =6 e x-(2 e) \\\\ y & =6 e x - 4 e + 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b20bc36-ce98-5edb-9e51-7375356be643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- 30 e \\left(x - \\frac{5}{6}\\right) + 1 + 5 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546032} {:answer/id #uuid "3bfaf069-a4d6-5131-a87d-a208594f2c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=30 e x + 1 + 5 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546034} {:answer/id #uuid "72048a0c-73ca-54b2-aa19-72248fb1e5b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5 e \\left(x - \\frac{5}{6}\\right) + 1 + 5 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546043} {:answer/id #uuid "750cf673-67dd-5803-97c5-520a46254372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=30 e \\left(x - \\frac{5}{6}\\right) + 5 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546045} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9790a2-417d-5f52-a1e3-b34bb0797907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=30 e x - 20 e + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb9790a2-417d-5f52-a1e3-b34bb0797907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=30 e x - 20 e + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546077} :answer-field/id #uuid "4506d1a2-b6f8-5a68-becb-87dba57a7620" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b32ededd-4f82-5701-b090-5a6b3fd40512" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=5{e}^{6x-4}+1$ at the point where $x=\\frac{5}{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{u}+1,\\,u=6x-4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=5{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =5{e}^{u}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =30{e}^{u} \\\\  & =30{e}^{6x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{5}{6}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=\\frac{5}{6}} & =30{e}^{6x-4}{|}_{x=\\frac{5}{6}} \\\\ & =30{e}^{(6)(\\frac{5}{6})-4} \\\\ & =30 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=x_1=\\frac{5}{6}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y_1 & =(5{e}^{6x-4}+1){|}_{x=\\frac{5}{6}} \\\\ & =5{e}^{(6)(\\frac{5}{6})-4}+1 \\\\ & =1 + 5 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(1 + 5 e) & =(30 e)(x-(\\frac{5}{6})) \\\\ y-(1 + 5 e) & =30 e x-(25 e) \\\\ y & =30 e x - 20 e + 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2b8c44-29d4-535a-ac99-52fb8107ea8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{s}{4}-\\frac{1}{3})(s+\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546275} {:answer/id #uuid "9446f1cc-7e0b-53a2-b234-d433ab653901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(s-\\frac{1}{6})(s+\\frac{1}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546578} {:answer/id #uuid "a5049ad1-06bf-512f-9dc8-6aa664a5b508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{s}{4}-\\frac{1}{9})(s+\\frac{1}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546619} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1cc090-da2f-589a-a3e1-c6264a798d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(s-\\frac{1}{9})(s+\\frac{1}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546677} {:answer/id #uuid "d12fc8ca-be1b-524b-92aa-34847c9803af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(s-\\frac{1}{3})(s+\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d12fc8ca-be1b-524b-92aa-34847c9803af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(s-\\frac{1}{3})(s+\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546714} :answer-field/id #uuid "0189ffec-539c-5f18-adf5-a8178ffb8b66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0e4d35de-1496-58dd-acc4-9d6da8717950" :question/math-academy-id "q-80009" :question/problem "Factor $\\frac{{s}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{36}.$" :question/worked-solution "Before we start our normal process of factoring, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $\\frac{1}{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{s}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{36}=\\frac{1}{4}({s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9})\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9} &= {s}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\ &= (s-\\frac{1}{3})(s+\\frac{1}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{s}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{36}=\\frac{1}{4}(s-\\frac{1}{3})(s+\\frac{1}{3})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dd51322-e8fb-5da6-8b86-a44dc4d96653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546529} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff63289-e195-5ca0-9c0f-60c6d613464e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546565} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a0b584-89d2-56d7-bceb-37505c98ad5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546727} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a62bcd-5c05-525d-941e-1f20d80183eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546731} {:answer/id #uuid "e9014057-f209-5b30-93b9-61fecfa103aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dd51322-e8fb-5da6-8b86-a44dc4d96653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546529} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffd11057-b685-55c9-893f-a00c1da187d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19d5c66d-e74b-5c3e-98da-49c6a7aecbf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-134399" :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{2,\\,1,\\,-1\\},\\,y=\\{15,\\,12,\\,9\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{2}{3},$ $\\overset{―}{y}=12,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}≈1.247,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}≈2.449.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The (population) linear correlation coefficient of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance, and ${\\sigma }_{x}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}$ are the standard deviations of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the covariance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(2-\\frac{2}{3})(15-12)+(1-\\frac{2}{3})(12-12)+(-1-\\frac{2}{3})(9-12)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(\\frac{4}{3})(3)+(\\frac{1}{3})(0)+(-\\frac{5}{3})(-3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[4+0+5] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{3}{1.247\\cdot 2.449} \\\\  & ≈0.98\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a3727ff-52bd-57c3-b177-f7a2d8ee75e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a3727ff-52bd-57c3-b177-f7a2d8ee75e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546596} :answer-field/id #uuid "1da0036b-025c-5d9f-a671-5ce1528b4da0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ac2a1ba-0f41-50b8-a45c-f89fbb85a481" :question/math-academy-id "q-352328" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f8ddb41c1b590eceeb3fb92c06bb1a34aa0b7ae92f9c2e76c5b6c595255086eb.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=2{e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2),$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2{e}^{\\theta }$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}(2{e}^{\\theta })^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}4{e}^{2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2}){|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\theta }\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52f3b306-9a74-5821-a7de-b9abe78cf733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52f3b306-9a74-5821-a7de-b9abe78cf733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546432} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b988de4-ecca-56c2-b6bc-fc57b5242fce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2194316f-56dc-52e2-8be5-47b08dc370f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-340478" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-4x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+4x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)]}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{15\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1-0)-\\frac{1}{10}(-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "304f73f4-0397-5854-960b-762988f4153d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546356} {:answer/id #uuid "62ec7766-2c11-5907-b71d-e497ceab6d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546467} {:answer/id #uuid "64e5f106-8480-5a83-97f5-e5174d418982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546473} {:answer/id #uuid "b56517bb-ec5e-584d-9307-e3e474bb3360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546657} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb85af9-dc03-5f44-8022-ff6dccdd0429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b56517bb-ec5e-584d-9307-e3e474bb3360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546657} :answer-field/id #uuid "abcaf86c-3cea-5f12-a174-9ce2866296f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2265fb13-ede5-5876-96c9-a4518515ab31" :question/math-academy-id "q-148337" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{9},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{5}{9},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.5329,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.2833,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{x+y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-(\\frac{11}{9})(\\frac{5}{9}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{81})}{(0.5329)\\cdot (0.2833)} \\\\  & ≈-0.08,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c2709fa-4adc-5605-a11a-8ab65411323d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2e-1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546345} {:answer/id #uuid "b389c4a4-9a5f-5726-baac-fe68902eb839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e+2}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546652} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ae699e-ca50-5532-b3f9-e8044519c63f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2e+1}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546654} {:answer/id #uuid "de3cc4ab-836f-59ce-b290-e93434f9fd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e-2}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546743} {:answer/id #uuid "f1417a6b-fec7-5120-8761-55733a3efa2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e-1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de3cc4ab-836f-59ce-b290-e93434f9fd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e-2}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546743} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f6244bc-3786-5afc-8ff3-37c064cb0ba6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2441fae2-b285-52be-9809-5b0ff2ea594e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114887" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {log}_{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{2}⁡x)(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{2}⁡x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{2}⁡x-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(x{log}_{2}⁡x-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D) \\\\  & =x{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2x{log}_{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{2x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}+\\frac{2x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}({ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{2D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}({ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}({ln}^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}({ln}^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-2}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3668cf4b-8050-5a96-996b-c27c89d6fa21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3668cf4b-8050-5a96-996b-c27c89d6fa21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546368} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0bed702-55a8-5fce-ad62-782cfcd3d3f5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546897} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6894baa-e048-55ba-9e2f-c9d22cb15c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6894baa-e048-55ba-9e2f-c9d22cb15c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546662} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc3c6a28-5baf-5cae-a10f-967bb181d649" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c14c3ab-0147-5311-bdf2-26e728ddcd5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-304084" :question/problem "Find where the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x}+x$ at the point  $(2,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe tangent intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+x.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-1})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-2}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{2}^{2}}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $x$-axis, we set $y=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+2 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-2\\cdot 2 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(-4,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "052b33f0-0879-5289-a4d7-39be855e9b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546258} {:answer/id #uuid "1a1765fc-6178-5a34-819c-f6621cb09c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}+2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546307} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2900c5-5719-539a-837b-df60e0071e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}-3\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546420} {:answer/id #uuid "5503e4a0-2bb6-52f2-a04d-816373cc0117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546439} {:answer/id #uuid "95fe2f50-e6ea-5cda-b510-d9b70073028f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5503e4a0-2bb6-52f2-a04d-816373cc0117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546439} :answer-field/id #uuid "af82264f-d4d2-5e87-919b-0a6badfef83f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f4365a0-c1aa-5cdc-8199-72e9694e40fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-114869" :question/problem "$\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2})(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546988 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05daf3d0-60c8-5258-8b72-1c240c8939ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.10\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546259} {:answer/id #uuid "3550769e-3a86-5731-8185-5eb11f50131c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.85\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546365} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c2f4b0-cd80-5104-a76d-a9103111aecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.45\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546616} {:answer/id #uuid "a664dc99-d82a-50a8-8008-5c999bea8255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.02\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546623} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb1cc1d-839e-54df-ab22-573f73ec9158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.65\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3c2f4b0-cd80-5104-a76d-a9103111aecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.45\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546616} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2296ad1-b5ad-5318-8ca2-47375c1f24ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3404cdf4-fad8-57cc-99a9-cffaef1b2f88" :question/math-academy-id "q-336839" :question/problem "A $500\\,\\text{L}$-capacity water tank initially holds $320\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $20\\,\\text{kg}$ of a dissolved contaminant. Clean water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $9\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed water flows out at a rate of $5\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Find, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg/min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $9\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $320\\,\\text{L}$ of water; clean water enters at $9\\,\\text{L/min},$ and mixed water leaves at $5\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\nV(t)=320+9t-5t=(320+4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the *concentration* of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $5\\,\\text{L/min},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{4(80+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{5Q}{4(80+t)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{5}{4(80+t)}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{5}{4(80+t)} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\,dQ & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{80+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|80+t|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\frac{A}{(80+t)^{5/4}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{A}{{80}^{5/4}} & =20 \\\\ A & =20(80)^{5/4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nQ(t)=20{(\\frac{80}{80+t})}^{5/4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. Since the tank has a capacity of $500\\,\\text{L},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =500 \\\\ 320+4{t}_{o} & =500 \\\\ 4{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =45\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(45)7 & =20{(\\frac{80}{80+45})}^{5/4} \\\\  & =20{(\\frac{80}{125})}^{5/4} \\\\  & ≈11.45\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ecfb71-5181-5349-90d0-aa801ee90c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546260} {:answer/id #uuid "15d9b08c-5b84-5d59-a03e-eb48b8bdcf05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546298} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc96e3f-b048-53f6-bbac-fc83895ed411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546311} {:answer/id #uuid "318edee1-713f-5f01-ae45-ef3fc7f1957c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546358} {:answer/id #uuid "75eeae67-abff-51e8-b38b-9678a6cab699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "318edee1-713f-5f01-ae45-ef3fc7f1957c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546358} :answer-field/id #uuid "f36d3cec-3da7-547e-beb8-441c3e66c73b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37a89027-b700-5936-918f-1cda621eae37" :question/math-academy-id "q-148419" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{7}{12},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.2898,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.2764,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot (\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{1}{2}xy)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{2}{3x}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{2}{3x}+\\frac{1}{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}+\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}+\\frac{3}{8})-(\\frac{3}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)(\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}-\\frac{7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{12})+(\\frac{3}{8}-\\frac{7}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{12}-\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{12}-\\frac{1}{16})}{(0.2898)\\cdot (0.2764)} \\\\  & ≈-0.06,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb26691a-385a-57d1-8078-1f35f18c73ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb26691a-385a-57d1-8078-1f35f18c73ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546669} :answer-field/id #uuid "767e5e84-d488-5271-8138-b5fbcff6b6b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "421776ee-f665-581f-82b5-c07ca0624bc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-340458" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-5x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-1-0)+\\frac{1}{14}(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547000 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93853bb0-6980-5ab5-ac47-eba906d1612f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93853bb0-6980-5ab5-ac47-eba906d1612f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546576} :answer-field/id #uuid "94e5d4f2-68cf-5189-be5f-0c89d4a9aed2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4a9b1203-e4ee-5057-a482-b299a09558e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-353098" :question/problem "![](images/da/dab5266e6607ac13c0cd9f7cd2dd58ada9659960beb596c19f7ba3e1c00cf953.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\frac{3}{2},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]:$\n\n$$\n\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/0b/0b5499cbbe756a5696e67178dfde8a11ea6a7f462fe2d6c17de6299dc621fdff.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=\\frac{3}{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\,{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\text{ and }\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}((3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\frac{9}{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\frac{9}{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{9}{4}\\theta +\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )){|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}))-(\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}))) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea6691d6-cc63-5f49-a64d-f75321108ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea6691d6-cc63-5f49-a64d-f75321108ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546779} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f3cb93-d5b9-5bb2-99da-ae2d6fb22250" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59e144f7-fb65-5ac7-82c8-6d786a6e59bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-340462" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-4x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+4x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)]}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{15\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1-0)+\\frac{1}{10}(-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547010 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb188b7-1bba-52cb-abb3-fd6aed751092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546280} {:answer/id #uuid "2544cee4-9901-5260-a310-d276e35ad5bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546333} {:answer/id #uuid "3735ad7d-4556-5e39-a71d-f32c39e15424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546370} {:answer/id #uuid "8720c01d-8df0-5742-be97-36d70ae63773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546550} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0d70e3-37d6-5e7a-96ef-4b4a41e04706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3735ad7d-4556-5e39-a71d-f32c39e15424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546370} :answer-field/id #uuid "84915d34-7d23-593e-8953-0481a5d952d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d902ef6-57b9-5fc3-a3a5-f189fbbd0124" :question/math-academy-id "q-61724" :question/problem "If $g(x)=M\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ and $g(4)=3,$ then $M=$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=4$ and $g(x)=3$ into the expression for $g(x)$ and solve for $M,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =M\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\ 3 & =M\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{4}}{{2}^{4}}) \\\\ 3 & =M\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 3\\cdot 16 & =M \\\\ 48 & =M\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $M=48.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fa57cc0-2c75-5aeb-aa4c-fd8435058a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546395} {:answer/id #uuid "4395965f-6000-5eb9-b9e6-b1775cbf5929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546404} {:answer/id #uuid "745dc55c-39f6-5188-893e-9e3610df2015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546508} {:answer/id #uuid "ad695ffc-4f3a-5a92-ab02-41059e526392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546638} {:answer/id #uuid "da5cda44-a757-5b8c-8598-b4c7d9c5616f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da5cda44-a757-5b8c-8598-b4c7d9c5616f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546733} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d79f823-7d5d-51a6-84fc-0f83ec6272a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f56c11c-a25d-5517-9da3-7d7d6457fb75" :question/math-academy-id "q-1929" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={log}_{5}⁡x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{5}⁡x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+5(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+\\frac{5}{x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dbf269c-de52-5987-8c5f-a5ab6a428860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546276} {:answer/id #uuid "3e34aae8-23b2-5c5e-b6cd-0140500f4a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546390} {:answer/id #uuid "548fd2f9-0377-54ba-80cc-2c84f01bfa2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546434} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c8562d-d0e1-528d-b700-ac3e947458d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546683} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f73334-b1a2-55a1-9029-06b428581140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8f73334-b1a2-55a1-9029-06b428581140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546807} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2b36089-1198-54b4-93f5-8abdffa58d09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6da1a8e2-af30-5f0e-8a87-d537d126dcee" :question/math-academy-id "q-148358" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)}{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.4332,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.4332,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician and integration by parts, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}xy\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)}{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[\\frac{\\pi }{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-y)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-y)-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-y)]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi +8}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi +8}{8}-(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}-8\\pi +16}{16} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-1)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-1)}^{2}}{(0.4332)\\cdot (0.4332)} \\\\  & ≈0.25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101aa237-9b05-5fcf-a6bc-d32593b1fcd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )}{9})}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546282} {:answer/id #uuid "56e8305c-4e37-5d71-9af2-df48c238587a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(1+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta )}{9})\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546442} {:answer/id #uuid "99f5835e-d85a-5ffb-b243-89bafa4277db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )}{3})}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546594} {:answer/id #uuid "aa679397-be5e-54b1-bb5c-f465e0cdfdfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )}{6})}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546635} {:answer/id #uuid "d38ee693-1c4f-52a9-9a51-5de7c1bf2804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )}{3})}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99f5835e-d85a-5ffb-b243-89bafa4277db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )}{3})}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546594} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ef4502-034c-55d0-a25e-422ca6738f37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7047c0ed-bfc1-51d7-b896-5a8dfa674a47" :question/math-academy-id "q-79796" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the total area enclosed by the graph of the polar curve $r=1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )}{3}$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )}{3}$, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )}{3})}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547018 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d0131b4-d374-5e74-aa90-c8abda4a45ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.50\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546347} {:answer/id #uuid "3001be2d-0b44-511f-939d-52802b97e714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.96\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546354} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8e3067-e0d5-573d-b2a5-05f7346c88a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.84\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546486} {:answer/id #uuid "74737ad0-325a-5620-b4cf-42637b26fb86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.31\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546509} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1829c7-cd54-5a8c-b939-0c1b1390639f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.98\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d0131b4-d374-5e74-aa90-c8abda4a45ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.50\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546347} :answer-field/id #uuid "32b609db-0cfa-50ce-be77-ccca954943ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72675a21-05f8-5d93-afac-bc69655e07ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-336678" :question/problem "A vat initially holds $500\\,\\text{L}$ of a sugar-water solution containing $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar.\nPure water is pumped into the vat at a rate of $14\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed sugar-water solution leaves the vat at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of sugar in the vat $14$ minutes after the water pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the vat at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the vat minus the rate at which sugar leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the vat.\n\nNote that pure water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of sugar) enters the vat at a rate of $14\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (14\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (14\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the vat.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the vat, we first determine the volume of solution in the vat at time $t.$\n\nThe vat initially contains $500\\,\\text{L}$ of solution; water enters at $14\\,\\text{L/min}$ and drains out at $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the vat after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n500+14t-10t=(500+4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{500+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed sugar-water solution leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{500+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{2(125+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{5Q}{2(125+t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{5}{2(125+t)}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{5}{2(125+t)} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{5}{2(125+t)})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{125+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|125+t|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|125+t|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(125+t)^{5/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=15,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =15 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(125+0)^{5/2}} & =15 \\\\ A & =15\\cdot {125}^{5/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=15{(\\frac{125}{125+t})}^{5/2}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of sugar in the vat $14$ minutes after the water is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(12)=15{(\\frac{125}{139})}^{5/2}≈11.50\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc0268ec-d8fa-5068-89a2-d047adff26bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "162" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc0268ec-d8fa-5068-89a2-d047adff26bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "162" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546737} :answer-field/id #uuid "a498868f-8302-53e1-96f6-a5c4ab372013" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a913f31-a39a-5842-bec8-7be0ed73cc2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-218599" :question/problem "If $f(x)=A\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ and $f(4)=2,$ then $A=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=4$ and $f(x)=2$ into the expression for $f(x)$ and solve for $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4} \\\\ 2 & =A\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{4}}{{3}^{4}}) \\\\ 2 & =A\\cdot \\frac{1}{81} \\\\ 2\\cdot 81 & =A \\\\ 162 & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=162.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1110af6f-ee8e-5716-bb31-7201313d5605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546285} {:answer/id #uuid "69f2cc06-4c6a-5ecd-8241-6b6fdbb489e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546483} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca9fba2-d820-5756-9477-e3b30e8c81f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546492} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff3df5c-e073-5d90-a4a7-c734d4432894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546564} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f2e5b8-6c24-59e1-81ab-09386b360221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1110af6f-ee8e-5716-bb31-7201313d5605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546285} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aa03162-aa27-5b75-80f4-0a2a94c5c65e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86de9def-10fe-5788-a466-3607900726ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-148421" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{12}(xy+1), & \\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1}{7},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.2619,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.4238,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{9}{12}(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{1}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x={y}^{2}}^{x=1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}y-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{8}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}{[\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{27}{y}^{9}-\\frac{1}{12}{y}^{6}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}[\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{27}-\\frac{1}{12}-(-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{27}-\\frac{1}{12})] \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}(\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{2}{27}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}-(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{315}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{(\\frac{8}{315})}{(0.2619)\\cdot (0.4238)} \\\\  & ≈0.23,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c1e0f93-78e7-591e-b13d-1e75cf7634cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10{e}^{3}-4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c1e0f93-78e7-591e-b13d-1e75cf7634cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10{e}^{3}-4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546383} :answer-field/id #uuid "e456e79c-9696-5fd5-914c-4b58ab494688" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "88eae5cb-fb52-512e-a423-9c404ac93779" :question/math-academy-id "q-346501" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{e}18{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=18{x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 18{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=6{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 18{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(6{x}^{3})-\\int (6{x}^{3})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-12\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 18{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =6{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-12\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-12(\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =6{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}-12D \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(6{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{4}{3})+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-12D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}18{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[{x}^{3}(6{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{4}{3})]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[{e}^{3}(6{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\frac{4}{3})]-[{1}^{3}(6{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{4}{3})] \\\\  & =[{e}^{3}(6-4+\\frac{4}{3})]-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10{e}^{3}-4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15014d3a-71e7-518b-aadf-e899615ac4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /9}^{\\pi /3}({sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )-{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\theta }{2}))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546295} {:answer/id #uuid "57cd0a2c-0f18-56ef-9c09-6fe9360e2b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /9}^{\\pi /3}({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\theta }{2})-{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546443} {:answer/id #uuid "80271ab7-037b-5aa4-93a3-ddb3b6db60be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}({sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\theta }{2}))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546538} {:answer/id #uuid "a9de8d93-59ba-5f2a-b0e0-c97127c5916b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}({sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )-{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\theta }{2}))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546633} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe0c52b-83e3-58fc-9db4-461500c15632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /9}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\theta }{2}))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15014d3a-71e7-518b-aadf-e899615ac4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /9}^{\\pi /3}({sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )-{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\theta }{2}))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546295} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ce70ab8-5bba-55a0-8772-84eb8dd3b36d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9596cbe8-4a33-51c1-84cf-edf4ceac0c56" :question/math-academy-id "q-125935" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b65eef55ba7db5252b4e472170ca699a7e21c2379648734101fa26bc456fa94.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$ and $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\theta }{2}),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: In the first quadrant, the curves intersect when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{9}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We are given that our curves intersect when ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{9}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/26/26c9070bb1406543061cf4072f0cab9162d63190f4d6ffc4601e88896a38a635.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\theta }{2}),$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ).$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{9},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /9}^{\\pi /3}({sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )-{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\theta }{2}))\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547044 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a5d5539-f19e-58d4-89ba-c26370b32e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(9{x}^{2}+6x+4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546378} {:answer/id #uuid "7e65fb00-30d6-59d7-ba38-99e4d7919af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(3{x}^{2}-15x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546530} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba2da88-7afb-5630-ab57-bfbfbca438f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(9{x}^{2}-6x+8)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546599} {:answer/id #uuid "c18beed8-cfd5-5a08-8fa1-a6733a3642d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{27}(9{x}^{2}-7x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546687} {:answer/id #uuid "f39741fc-058e-5d16-9fa8-ead8b1d6a65b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{27}(3{x}^{2}+7x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ba2da88-7afb-5630-ab57-bfbfbca438f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(9{x}^{2}-6x+8)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546599} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8ae469c-ade7-5edf-add1-d9b620e0dc4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b68d5307-5a40-5cd7-a13f-e872bf3c3818" :question/math-academy-id "q-14739" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int (3{x}^{2}+2){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3{x}^{2}+2\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{x}^{2}+2){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}+2)(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3})(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{x}^{2}+2){e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{6}{3}\\int x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{x}^{2}+2){e}^{3x}}{3}-2\\int x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3})(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(3x-1)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{x}^{2}+2){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(3{x}^{2}+2){e}^{3x}}{3}-2(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(3x-1)+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{x}^{2}+2){e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{2{e}^{3x}}{9}(3x-1)-2D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(3(3{x}^{2}+2)-2(3x-1))-2D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(9{x}^{2}-6x+8)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25a922f4-2d9d-5871-8561-90a11f1180f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546335} {:answer/id #uuid "333ff7b8-358e-5ce3-b123-bd1e014088ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546363} {:answer/id #uuid "5faa619c-acfc-59d4-95ba-cf127af6cc6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546457} {:answer/id #uuid "9a52084e-204b-5a20-b7fa-46763ae8012e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546597} {:answer/id #uuid "dea2d307-2cdd-5b5e-97e7-7d1ce5fb5e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5faa619c-acfc-59d4-95ba-cf127af6cc6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546457} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3718fae-c042-5c2d-a11b-debd358108e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b7ebc1ef-47dd-5be6-aa58-7b0c8c511acd" :question/math-academy-id "q-49929" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}4({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={z}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=2z, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}=4{e}^{2z}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z=4\\int {e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z=2{e}^{2z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =({z}^{2}+1)(2{e}^{2z})-\\int (2{e}^{2z})(2z)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}-4\\int z{e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int z{e}^{2z}\\text{d}z.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=z\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}={e}^{2z}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z=\\int {e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z=\\frac{{e}^{2z}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int z{e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(z)(\\frac{{e}^{2z}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2z}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{z{e}^{2z}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{z{e}^{2z}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2z}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z & =2({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}-4\\int z{e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}-4(\\frac{z{e}^{2z}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2z}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =2({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}-2z{e}^{2z}+{e}^{2z}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-4D.$ \n\nFinally, then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z & =(2({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}-2z{e}^{2z}+{e}^{2z}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2({1}^{2}+1){(e)}^{2}-2(1)^{2}{e}^{2}+{e}^{2})-(2(0+1){e}^{0}-2\\cdot 0\\cdot {e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1159e19b-92b6-5620-82f3-d1ce2d139b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1159e19b-92b6-5620-82f3-d1ce2d139b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546286} :answer-field/id #uuid "28ead3db-6f6f-5655-b675-4d6076ef9360" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b966907d-654c-5943-b963-c5a2c298faf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-353129" :question/problem "![](images/3d/3da35964a3877c7a0b3fce7e4f8c2db2d320f1d69f720541afa5dd1428447b73.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]:$\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/dd/dd265c911aa4fc5713b0e53cea2ee751e84cd7c54b2b2aefb83bfae8d1f7d92f.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}(4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -(4-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-2(-\\sqrt{3}+\\frac{5\\pi }{6})+2(\\sqrt{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547049 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52851b61-8b9c-53cc-91c6-5200fa78f1ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546430} {:answer/id #uuid "6f2ba34b-4251-5e97-822e-7d6f89fec290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546498} {:answer/id #uuid "71e229f8-046f-5d88-8cf3-15d0ca09c39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546502} {:answer/id #uuid "a47b0af8-b237-5b70-8f37-b67b00a01cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546618} {:answer/id #uuid "ea372be5-00ac-5d06-b324-96a9d214c32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea372be5-00ac-5d06-b324-96a9d214c32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546777} :answer-field/id #uuid "60100382-bef0-5d40-8e4e-57031710307e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bc404082-c599-5306-899d-26fb5a707805" :question/math-academy-id "q-114875" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})\\cdot 1\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-3\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-3(-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-3D.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{3(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )}{4}-(\\frac{3(0)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)}{2}+\\frac{3(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{2}-\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)}{4}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{3\\pi (-1)}{4}-0-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebb827d1-06d8-5d42-8723-79e381e48913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebb827d1-06d8-5d42-8723-79e381e48913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546783} :answer-field/id #uuid "51149079-3dcc-5052-8b68-1b2d681e947d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7c751ec-42d9-5e30-a955-bbbf2b18ef36" :question/math-academy-id "q-175691" :question/problem "If $k=\\frac{21-3k}{4},$ then $k=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{21-3k}{4} \\\\ \\frac{21-3k}{4} & =k \\\\ 4(\\frac{21-3k}{4}) & =4\\cdot k \\\\ \\cancel{4}(\\frac{21-3k}{\\cancel{4}}) & =4k \\\\ 21-3k & =4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21-3k & =4k \\\\ 21-3k+3k & =4k+3k \\\\ 21 & =7k \\\\ \\frac{21}{7} & =\\frac{7k}{7} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{\\cancel{7}k}{\\cancel{7}} \\\\ 3 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbfe3030-34aa-5743-844c-793f65847a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbfe3030-34aa-5743-844c-793f65847a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546736} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae0782a8-a8ce-5695-a710-1fed57c8a4e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ceab1153-db5f-5530-aee8-9a4937774de9" :question/math-academy-id "q-344849" :question/problem "Find the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{|x-1|}+2$ and the point $P(2,2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{|x-1|}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{|x-1|}+2-2)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+|x-1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1,$ we have $|x-1|=x-1,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+x-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+x-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{3}{2})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-3(\\frac{3}{2})+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}-\\frac{9}{2}+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547060 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32660273-053d-57c2-8679-1174253ff668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32660273-053d-57c2-8679-1174253ff668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546360} :answer-field/id #uuid "691cc2fe-d17d-54b1-9079-15c5e5316c91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4d3424f-8de3-594c-bbdf-8f7aff226934" :question/math-academy-id "q-340461" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x-3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-1)+\\frac{1}{16}(0-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116e1223-c9c0-50e1-9796-7612d14b69d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546288} {:answer/id #uuid "3af52246-e75a-5df3-a2e8-127297df4308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546380} {:answer/id #uuid "cee2766c-0382-569e-bb67-a703ad240c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546712} {:answer/id #uuid "e3bf9b68-0dfc-54cc-a6bb-ef6cd1976695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546759} {:answer/id #uuid "fc396241-6675-55dc-9a31-e2e02c8f817b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "116e1223-c9c0-50e1-9796-7612d14b69d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546288} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9e55990-2ad1-513e-bcc2-f70980e885ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db6e411e-8a97-527f-83cb-17311240c8a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-148422" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}{\\pi }, & \\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{8}{5\\pi },\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{8}{5\\pi },\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.2719,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.2719,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}xy\\cdot \\frac{8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }\\text{ }{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}({x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{1}{4}{(1-{y}^{2})}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}(1-{y}^{2}){y}^{3}]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}[(1-2{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})y+2(1-{y}^{2}){y}^{3}]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }-(\\frac{8}{5\\pi })(\\frac{8}{5\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{50\\pi -192}{75{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{(\\frac{50\\pi -192}{75{\\pi }^{2}})}{(0.2719)\\cdot (0.2719)} \\\\  & ≈-0.64,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a0b7907-741b-5293-b278-a9e4a5f33e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a0b7907-741b-5293-b278-a9e4a5f33e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546375} :answer-field/id #uuid "b96d03be-bed3-558c-a1ef-99668bcb5dc0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc83095a-e05b-5bdd-9ae4-88a129415b52" :question/math-academy-id "q-340459" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(0-0)+\\frac{1}{12}(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a3e00e5-36ae-5b66-9e13-738ec9051511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a3e00e5-36ae-5b66-9e13-738ec9051511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546484} :answer-field/id #uuid "63bcb4a5-fa6f-50b8-b5ef-841210199229" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e84cbeff-b411-5590-9b42-9e298fadb428" :question/math-academy-id "q-353104" :question/problem "![](images/af/af57b718e46c7ce0c99f4ec49a3e6903e31996ee5373556357f972deb604f6f0.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]:$\n\n$$\n\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/47/47d82588a97e539328dd60a48e07e78d30b020ca1da7a0bf9c091148c18b5d73.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\,{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\text{ and }\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3}{2}\\theta +\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547073 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16b56f03-d3ba-5b8a-beda-1ede1217e4eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{27}(3{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+5)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546301} {:answer/id #uuid "29523a5f-ea36-5d82-b658-db73edef63bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{27}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+8)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546341} {:answer/id #uuid "4c335eff-2b6b-524d-90cf-5442e47c7a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{9}(3{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546421} {:answer/id #uuid "54e90af3-fa72-54c7-92dc-5200072bed0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}({(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}-4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546438} {:answer/id #uuid "b864fa64-c785-5bce-9f26-627200acd91a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{27}(6{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29523a5f-ea36-5d82-b658-db73edef63bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{27}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+8)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546341} :answer-field/id #uuid "4681467c-286a-5fcb-8e52-ae9fe0e595eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eec4d3ca-fb39-5950-ae42-a7772eee1331" :question/math-academy-id "q-50230" :question/problem "$\\int 4{x}^{2}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x=\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4{x}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{x}^{2}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{16}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}))(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{x}^{2}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{16}{3}(\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{16{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{9}+\\frac{32{x}^{3}}{27}-\\frac{16D}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{27}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+8)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhere, $C=-\\frac{16D}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "488049cf-e3a2-5c9c-8f46-aed73cfafe21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546412} {:answer/id #uuid "64921934-9675-5052-a0c4-b17d8a0e63de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546471} {:answer/id #uuid "65abe717-6ba1-508b-833d-8fd0d40688c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546474} {:answer/id #uuid "96db19c3-f95b-5250-9789-c0c946a9db43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546585} {:answer/id #uuid "bd051110-3f71-5b72-82c0-c4da6900120b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64921934-9675-5052-a0c4-b17d8a0e63de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546471} :answer-field/id #uuid "84855aea-9637-57da-bba7-9e0bb4cd91ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "efd2d9b0-0b23-5763-b3ea-5ce0374b01c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126402" :question/problem "![](images/97/9798487c945153400cf573ab87361d316c21a9040c1e17adf16051843bc9acd3.png)\n\nFind the area of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]:$\n\n$$\n\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/33/336b0f0bb8c1d5bba38d2fa5b40a09fb9c76fb4cdd7db21d04ceb14c0816aa52.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}((\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -(3-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(-3+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-3\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-3(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+2\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-\\pi +2\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c7cd11f-6a9e-530a-85f2-832baf9ebcb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=24{(\\frac{90}{90+t})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546385} {:answer/id #uuid "5535e146-fa65-5186-a97a-50884375c362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=18\\frac{90}{(90+t)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546440} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6a8521-cadb-550b-9b3c-ad524ce80812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=24{(\\frac{9}{90+t})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546493} {:answer/id #uuid "e35c7ab7-4248-5c5f-88c0-06d8707fb470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=18{(\\frac{90}{90+t})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546757} {:answer/id #uuid "fb7fcf0b-cbef-5a6f-b78e-83e21545eeb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=1800{(\\frac{9}{90+t})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e35c7ab7-4248-5c5f-88c0-06d8707fb470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=18{(\\frac{90}{90+t})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546757} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f739ba7-4312-53fa-a7d7-8241e430155b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f484bd1c-f53f-587d-99ce-e7d9cbfcb8f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-336663" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $450\\,\\text{gal}$ of a sugar-water solution containing $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of sugar.\nFresh water is poured into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed sugar solution leaves the tank at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nFind an equation for the amount of sugar $Q(t),$ in pounds, in the tank $t$ minutes after the fresh water is first added." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of sugar) enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $450\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution.\n\n- Fresh water enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $15t\\,\\text{gal}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $10t\\,\\text{gal}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n450+15t-10t=(450+5t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{450+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{450+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{2Q}{90+t} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{90+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{90+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{90+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(90+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(90+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ A & =18\\cdot {90}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=18{(\\frac{90}{90+t})}^{2}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "133d1c36-6fc4-54a5-886d-ffa6d1d901d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi -4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546291} {:answer/id #uuid "594935a6-2810-554d-9799-095ccc4adcbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi -8}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546446} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3b55a9-801e-5581-82ba-197cfadd134c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi +8}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546528} {:answer/id #uuid "c8201213-aa6a-595a-9cb4-25b97aefe536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -8}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546695} {:answer/id #uuid "c84f9e5b-b609-56b9-a3cf-c70887165e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi +2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "594935a6-2810-554d-9799-095ccc4adcbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi -8}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546446} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6eb1304-86d3-5439-8c1a-6e535052759f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fea4b600-1957-5ee7-b24c-2a8da664ed40" :question/math-academy-id "q-123914" :question/problem "Find the area bounded by the polar curve $r=2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(4-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }-2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(-1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }-2(-\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi })) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+2\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =3\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =3(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2})+4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+4\\cdot (-1)-\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi -8}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db60baf4-1b59-55ef-a8fb-c9c833a2a2eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db60baf4-1b59-55ef-a8fb-c9c833a2a2eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547260} :answer-field/id #uuid "058f07c8-5f73-5a3c-aeae-eb374917bfeb" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547196} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2452da6f-cdc9-5872-a3d3-7412163e8aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2452da6f-cdc9-5872-a3d3-7412163e8aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547209} :answer-field/id #uuid "412100a7-eb52-50c4-8414-e33946395a31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547219} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6033715-e2fe-511c-9dbb-8190d0c9f015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6033715-e2fe-511c-9dbb-8190d0c9f015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547259} :answer-field/id #uuid "4359664d-b09b-56da-95ca-f01a0276cd0a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547220} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66faaf71-7d3a-5990-aa4f-9631c0b2bda5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66faaf71-7d3a-5990-aa4f-9631c0b2bda5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547230} :answer-field/id #uuid "acc860e0-502c-5c19-ae2e-737167082c1a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3310d24c-d7fc-5ec9-92b8-98deb7cbc7e4" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{2}{3}L+2A$, where\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$L=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nand\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{2} & 3 & - \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\frac{2}{3}L+2A=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$-9$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$2$</td><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $L$ by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and each entry of $A$ by $2,$ and then add the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}L+2A & =\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{2} & 3 & - \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3) & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3) & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-9) \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 18 & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3) & \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot (- \\frac{3}{2}) & 2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (- \\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ 2\\cdot (-3) & 2\\cdot 2 & 2\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2-3 & -2+6 & -6-3 \\\\ 12-6 & -2+4 & 0+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 & -9 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fcd25c1-b9a9-56eb-b404-446e379f595c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547378} {:answer/id #uuid "689eccb5-cccc-5071-a58b-3df40833a9ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547547} {:answer/id #uuid "8c692d74-d882-579c-a263-2ba81452e19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547625} {:answer/id #uuid "a6af4e95-a3bd-547c-96b0-c9157f6909e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547683} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e40d2d-f0bc-584c-b6d9-b4b71e9295ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fcd25c1-b9a9-56eb-b404-446e379f595c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547378} :answer-field/id #uuid "56ebf041-55c8-5fe4-affb-3c90d8aaeea7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0515d331-a2ed-5378-ab7b-e5eca8ff6d75" :question/math-academy-id "q-78590" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $3.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{3},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{4}{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{3}=\\frac{4}{9}(\\frac{27}{64})=\\frac{3}{16} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{4}{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{4}=\\frac{4}{9}(\\frac{81}{256})=\\frac{9}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{(\\frac{9}{64})}{(\\frac{3}{16})}=\\frac{9}{64}\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{16})}{(1-\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{16})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0340ba7d-60fe-5ce5-94fe-c3642864b856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}({e}^{-\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547323} {:answer/id #uuid "200d0628-556e-5baa-ac44-a3d113c85b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547379} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3d7672-ea17-5f1b-946d-cbc34bf9be65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-2\\pi }-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547560} {:answer/id #uuid "99e7c620-6fc1-5713-836c-454ba4907213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-\\pi }+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547652} {:answer/id #uuid "b036bbd1-1c23-5d6d-9a9d-9e351a182b4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}({e}^{-2\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99e7c620-6fc1-5713-836c-454ba4907213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-\\pi }+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547652} :answer-field/id #uuid "2abcc4ca-4f5a-5ee4-b3d4-9f4549d9ca5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b786a85-3015-50d7-b6da-f38d91ed719a" :question/math-academy-id "q-115003" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2J \\\\ I & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2(-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{-2\\pi /2}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})))-(\\frac{{e}^{-2(0)}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{5}+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{-\\pi }+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c033b7b5-f0bb-5912-ac93-d7393c96dac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c033b7b5-f0bb-5912-ac93-d7393c96dac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547729} :answer-field/id #uuid "1146e643-4cd0-54ef-a3e2-eff97703cea4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d4ef9d9-890a-5da6-8625-b613890df574" :question/math-academy-id "q-348879" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{4}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{ln}^{3}⁡x}{x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{6x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{6x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{4}⁡x)(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{6})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{6})(\\frac{4{ln}^{3}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{6}-4\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{6}-4I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{6}-4I \\\\ 5I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{6} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{6x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{30}({ln}^{5}⁡x)\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}({ln}^{5}⁡({e}^{3})-{ln}^{5}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}((3\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{5}-{0}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}({3}^{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff5d37fa-afb4-58b9-ac62-05838ab2df59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{625}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff5d37fa-afb4-58b9-ac62-05838ab2df59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{625}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547842} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3a039a4-2ad9-58c4-9de9-69c93ba57a90" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d5e523f-cb45-5cb6-96e4-37ea7521523c" :question/math-academy-id "q-348848" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{5}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{5}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{4}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{ln}^{3}⁡x}{x}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{3x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{4}⁡x)(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3})(\\frac{4{ln}^{3}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{3}-4\\int \\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{3}-4I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{3}-4I \\\\ 5I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{3} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{5}⁡x}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{5}}\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡x}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{15}({ln}^{5}⁡x)\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}({ln}^{5}⁡({e}^{5})-{ln}^{5}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}((5\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{5}-{0}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}({5}^{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547988 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e5f1481-f060-5e2c-a1ba-9d650eb0364e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547410} {:answer/id #uuid "3de57636-1ffa-509b-8522-11db772f0631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547454} {:answer/id #uuid "6cede503-5875-5d7e-acae-646cdb24f91a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547558} {:answer/id #uuid "8abaac36-eba4-5279-995b-1ce237e32fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547619} {:answer/id #uuid "c80ff131-1ac8-5344-98c3-286bacff57ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e5f1481-f060-5e2c-a1ba-9d650eb0364e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547410} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6ff2439-5d4f-5486-b23d-8199292dfe50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "204edd9f-19f2-513e-ac33-9db65e721229" :question/math-academy-id "q-12924" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548000 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a066f3a-c9ff-5381-9be1-463aeaf34b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547479} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2bfd91-8772-51d9-bdc2-da46a676b608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547516} {:answer/id #uuid "718f587f-9400-5ff2-8042-b2bbeeac6f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547569} {:answer/id #uuid "8782a4d4-81e3-5eb0-85bd-15bc8474d851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547608} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6fa4fc-9ef6-5b97-80a9-85f5119427ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8782a4d4-81e3-5eb0-85bd-15bc8474d851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547608} :answer-field/id #uuid "76f810c9-6c36-550e-ba2c-f211b1f812da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4bc6d1d2-09ee-5cec-a2ab-850e837a407d" :question/math-academy-id "q-149608" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expected value of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2y(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =2y(1-0) \\\\  & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot 2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108be319-2cac-5831-9175-1aabb3c72448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "108be319-2cac-5831-9175-1aabb3c72448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547351} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1a4447f-3bfa-5239-8811-1f6dff980305" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c0fb001-b215-5979-89f5-05a01ae52266" :question/math-academy-id "q-350641" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2n}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2(1)}=\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2(2)}=\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}}{\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}={(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{4}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{1}{9})}{(1-\\frac{1}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(\\frac{8}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{9}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66021cb4-d4dc-5536-b740-cb112f549955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{25} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{1}{80}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547542} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab77c8f-f65f-5385-b65d-d3ff9200abe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{75} & \\frac{1}{120} \\\\ \\frac{1}{120} & \\frac{1}{80}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547618} {:answer/id #uuid "8bea8944-aeb8-56ab-bc44-a9115f9d6b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{75} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547621} {:answer/id #uuid "c0675adb-146f-509c-9662-380def9fc9b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{50} & \\frac{1}{120} \\\\ \\frac{1}{120} & \\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547730} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4fcec9-3717-5441-ad16-3b9193cd5316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{75} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{3}{20}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ab77c8f-f65f-5385-b65d-d3ff9200abe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{75} & \\frac{1}{120} \\\\ \\frac{1}{120} & \\frac{1}{80}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547618} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcc58af9-a17d-54ac-a6d0-c02b4698e66a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f28747a-e241-5f89-b726-699a85c63f84" :question/math-academy-id "q-159419" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}48y(x-y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{4}{5},$ $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1}{4},$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{2}{3},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\text{Var}[X].$ Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}48{y}^{3}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}(x{y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{y=0}^{y=x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{160}{x}^{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48\\cdot \\frac{3}{320}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{20}{[\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{40}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{80}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}xy\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}48x{y}^{2}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-x{y}^{3})\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48\\cdot \\frac{5}{192}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{[\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}-(\\frac{4}{5})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{120}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{75} & \\frac{1}{120} \\\\ \\frac{1}{120} & \\frac{1}{80}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec2f68f-3a5c-5320-80df-4677e1be6e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }-1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547347} {:answer/id #uuid "6081e439-1860-5222-91b9-aee228c648b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }+2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547525} {:answer/id #uuid "842dae31-aac6-5e10-b497-9abe5c499c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi /2}-1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547599} {:answer/id #uuid "8bff236b-251c-5407-acdf-108c7f7dd322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /2}+1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547623} {:answer/id #uuid "c07201a8-c8e4-5b0a-831a-ee4bce670816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi /2}+1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bff236b-251c-5407-acdf-108c7f7dd322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /2}+1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547623} :answer-field/id #uuid "2392f4ba-bd38-5a07-8e70-90353132c90f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "509669b5-ef46-5eb1-8d54-7bbaca5f8fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-115002" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}I & =\\frac{2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})))-(\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-0}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0))-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(0)))) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /2}}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /2}+1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e77a6f5-b85a-52a5-a006-9b7d292f2cc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{401}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547345} {:answer/id #uuid "13ad4530-88a4-53d4-8e25-b235bdfe115f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{209}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547355} {:answer/id #uuid "16ac0b96-91a3-5dfc-b5ef-29cbc766835a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{417}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547364} {:answer/id #uuid "80d7caca-10f2-5451-a96d-795017485bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{449}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547591} {:answer/id #uuid "92e2c5b9-18da-53a6-8dd8-6cb2c77bfba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{401}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16ac0b96-91a3-5dfc-b5ef-29cbc766835a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{417}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547364} :answer-field/id #uuid "69965209-526e-5658-899a-44e6dd8b07be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "510f41a7-5712-55db-b50d-5c80967bb9fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-12889" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4)}^{4}=A{x}^{8}+B{x}^{6}+C{x}^{4}+128{x}^{2}+256\n$$\n\nwhere $A,B$ and $C$ are constants, find the value of $A+B+C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},$ and $b=4.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4)}^{4} & =1{a}^{4}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})}^{4}(4)^{0}+4{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})}^{3}{(4)}^{1}+6{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})}^{2}{(4)}^{2} \\\\  & \\,+4{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})}^{1}{(4)}^{3}+1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})}^{0}{(4)}^{4} \\\\  & =1(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{16})(1)+4(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{8})(4)+6(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})(16) \\\\  & \\,+4(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})(64)+1(1)(256) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{8}}{16}+\\frac{16{x}^{6}}{8}+\\frac{96{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{256{x}^{2}}{2}+256 \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{8}}{16}+2{x}^{6}+24{x}^{4}+128{x}^{2}+256.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $A=\\frac{1}{16},B=2$ and $C=24.$ So, finally\n\n$$\nA+B+C=\\frac{1}{16}+2+24=\\frac{417}{16}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04876404-2581-5642-a614-ac8532d94ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{51}{80}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547326} {:answer/id #uuid "28e7f859-7126-5457-a4e0-c1a1e1d2204b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{11}{40}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547390} {:answer/id #uuid "32e2ee64-527c-5c83-b039-b6ea6f47e219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{23}{40}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547422} {:answer/id #uuid "f06f1240-944d-5a7e-801d-c1580106d19c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{23}{40}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547811} {:answer/id #uuid "f530e59e-19da-56aa-a5f6-c0afac7fd1bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{11}{40}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04876404-2581-5642-a614-ac8532d94ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{51}{80}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547326} :answer-field/id #uuid "3653cd9d-fe12-51a1-8be3-fd825c0586b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53e9c56c-8467-523e-bd98-6ac7236cb57e" :question/math-academy-id "q-159402" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\, & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,-1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=0$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{5}{4},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{4}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-{(0)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{33}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5}-{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{80}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$ The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle\n\n$$\nS=[-1,1]\\times [-1,2].\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}$ and $h(y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}.$\n\nHence, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{51}{80}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c09fbcf-e1dc-5950-9508-6766eb430f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{45} & \\frac{5}{63} \\\\ \\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{67}{548}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547447} {:answer/id #uuid "8486b2b4-5627-54ae-9bfc-12a68a4ca561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{1}{21} \\\\ \\frac{1}{21} & \\frac{56}{843}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547600} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3456dc-3ee8-5bd4-b669-70e0e8adddde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{21} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{760}{1\\,323}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547753} {:answer/id #uuid "ce082f30-4d9c-529e-8724-6a216a95f170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{5}{63} \\\\ \\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{760}{1\\,323}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547758} {:answer/id #uuid "ff923f25-09d1-59df-bac6-3e88c64bc811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{45} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{56}{843}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce082f30-4d9c-529e-8724-6a216a95f170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{5}{63} \\\\ \\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{760}{1\\,323}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547758} :answer-field/id #uuid "3850f8d6-6285-5466-abaf-4c01feeefec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7039fb00-3d4a-54d6-a4b0-1bcf58d49ef2" :question/math-academy-id "q-159422" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{16}({x}^{2}-y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{5}{3},$ $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{20}{21},$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=\\frac{40}{27},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\text{Var}[X].$\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\frac{5}{16}{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}({x}^{4}-y{x}^{2})\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[y{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{x}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{32}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{6}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{32}{[\\frac{1}{7}{x}^{7}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{7}-{(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{63}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{Var}[Y].$ Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{27}-{(\\frac{20}{21})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{760}{1\\,323}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}xy\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\frac{5}{16}xy({x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}x]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{7})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{7}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{96}{[\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{8}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}-(\\frac{5}{3})\\cdot (\\frac{20}{21}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{63}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{5}{63} \\\\ \\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{760}{1\\,323}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5afcd8-4cf8-5778-a410-d996de8caa7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547343} {:answer/id #uuid "3b943b2d-ea0b-5325-b2aa-3e8653e1e477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547444} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef15147-7c05-56d2-a62e-10a55dfa362d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547562} {:answer/id #uuid "a306fe67-74bc-5773-80ce-c1513aa6afc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547673} {:answer/id #uuid "e32c4540-3e5c-5944-a86d-c76ecf3c6add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a306fe67-74bc-5773-80ce-c1513aa6afc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547673} :answer-field/id #uuid "be9f989d-d56a-54a6-a664-496350494e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ac43aed-fdf1-5439-85aa-b688b91d01e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124590" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{25}{27}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $3.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{3},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =\\frac{25}{27}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{25}{27}(\\frac{27}{125})=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{25}{27}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{4}=\\frac{25}{27}(\\frac{81}{625})=\\frac{3}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{25})}{(\\frac{1}{5})}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{25}{27}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5})}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19a8a941-abee-5eec-a85d-2c36090c0c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2{e}^{\\theta }(\\theta +1)+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547369} {:answer/id #uuid "829b49d5-b322-53c3-b0f4-1c6304fa46ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}-2{e}^{\\theta }-2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547594} {:answer/id #uuid "c107c606-2649-543d-89fe-4a7928489ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}-2{e}^{\\theta }(1-\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547735} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d84b89-ed7c-5866-bbe7-886aa45f00d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2{e}^{\\theta }(\\theta +1)+2{e}^{2\\theta }+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547741} {:answer/id #uuid "fceb4866-634d-53f1-b923-46c9e70b07f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta {e}^{\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4d84b89-ed7c-5866-bbe7-886aa45f00d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2{e}^{\\theta }(\\theta +1)+2{e}^{2\\theta }+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547741} :answer-field/id #uuid "17a97da4-f06a-512c-b0f2-d273f0e4876b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8023cebc-048b-5288-9c32-1da095db126f" :question/math-academy-id "q-103827" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }$ for $-\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta +{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=1+{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\theta +{e}^{\\theta })^{2}+(1+{e}^{\\theta })^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta {e}^{\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }+1+2{e}^{\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2{e}^{\\theta }(\\theta +1)+2{e}^{2\\theta }+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548022 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2185bcec-d33c-5b85-a90f-3baf6d8e3134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547385} {:answer/id #uuid "28fce1c0-fdae-52e2-b558-861fa662bf1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547391} {:answer/id #uuid "5abc1e52-5007-58cf-923d-9f9cdea5bf21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547518} {:answer/id #uuid "5e40558d-2c08-52ce-bd22-49714e884a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547522} {:answer/id #uuid "69067c60-3e9e-5a7c-8919-986fa0b1106b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69067c60-3e9e-5a7c-8919-986fa0b1106b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547550} :answer-field/id #uuid "da4c8753-aa62-5b12-846b-3fa944adeb01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84522aa8-0251-51f3-8f96-c1848baee4b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-67300" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}=5.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}{10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548033 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34950a5f-0e45-502b-a869-e016971bd6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{8}{75}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547425} {:answer/id #uuid "4c028ae0-4e46-5185-bea4-b79e305dc544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{8}{75}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547489} {:answer/id #uuid "4dfa5556-a2dc-5232-9d7f-e3796a0407dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9} & -1 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{8}{75}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547494} {:answer/id #uuid "8675456c-15df-5d06-a100-2d7b178a0c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{25}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547606} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc276fa-92de-5c9e-be25-aa6ec3df6cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18} & -1 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{4}{25}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34950a5f-0e45-502b-a869-e016971bd6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{8}{75}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547425} :answer-field/id #uuid "294a6c0d-e8c4-539b-87eb-6c2be022c57a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9895539f-08a9-52d3-aba6-0550c7a4d108" :question/math-academy-id "q-159407" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{3},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{8}{5},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{3})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{3})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{5}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{6}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}-{(\\frac{8}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$ The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle\n\n$$\nS=[0,1]\\times [0,2].\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)=2x$ and $h(y)=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}.$\n\nHence, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{8}{75}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a94de121-a4bc-5c49-b381-af42e7f0741f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a94de121-a4bc-5c49-b381-af42e7f0741f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547689} :answer-field/id #uuid "72a14dfe-4aac-5b96-99e1-2adb2bdf2921" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4bf276d-b40b-5d47-b840-e4ace9d966fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-126763" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{″}(0).$\n\n${f}^{″}(0)\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}+\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\  & =2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\cdot 2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}+2\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4{x}^{2}y+2\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the point $(0,0)$ lies on $f,$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,0)} & =-4\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot 0+2\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548044 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3208f809-5202-519c-a4c1-b2c071b30e1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.78$ grams" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547420} {:answer/id #uuid "3633d1c4-a4bd-5d6c-9a1e-fe155268f04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.82$ grams" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547428} {:answer/id #uuid "70486f57-d8c6-5d88-9e76-73287f23fb7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.75$ grams" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547567} {:answer/id #uuid "b360a2fc-6825-56f7-a4ae-202963cd606a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.8$ grams" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547706} {:answer/id #uuid "bf33ba30-f07a-5c5e-8673-051f5a7fed51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.85$ grams" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3208f809-5202-519c-a4c1-b2c071b30e1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.78$ grams" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547420} :answer-field/id #uuid "c889ce98-c36c-5100-9ccc-2833887780d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b642f61b-90d3-52e8-a698-ed0e6fdd52ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-132849" :question/problem "In a factory, the average amount of sugar in a cookie is $15$ grams. However, $10%$ of cookies contain no more than $14$ grams of sugar. Assuming that the amount of sugar in each cookie is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the amount of sugar in a cookie, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the amount (in grams) of sugar in a cookie. Then we have $X∼N(15,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 14)=0.10.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 14) & =0.10 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{14-15}{\\sigma }) & =0.10 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{1}{\\sigma }) & =0.10 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.10)=-1.2816.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =-1.2816 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =1.2816 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{1.2816} \\\\  & ≈0.78.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c5745e4-3ad7-502f-bce7-234772762acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547448} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbc9f32-3c2a-5c31-81b2-2d34ba3625e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547521} {:answer/id #uuid "6771ee98-f25a-5cdc-91d7-e1d71d8eda3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547546} {:answer/id #uuid "83542680-0c0f-5bce-90df-2f3546c7a3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547596} {:answer/id #uuid "b01056a8-96d8-5259-85d4-db47041ed857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6771ee98-f25a-5cdc-91d7-e1d71d8eda3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547546} :answer-field/id #uuid "caa5996b-3602-50fb-816f-2fafeaae3fc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b82a91db-40b3-51bf-b539-d94cf7913732" :question/math-academy-id "q-146284" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=4]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|4}=\\frac{3}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=4] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)]-{\\mu }_{X|4}^{2} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}+{2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+{3}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}]-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a44699f-24c8-5a58-ba2d-76fbbc86ccec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{7}⁡x}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547517} {:answer/id #uuid "7b7a4366-3be3-5b98-9a3e-ba4a656574ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547583} {:answer/id #uuid "8e73f739-d875-5435-8483-802c12bd03ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547633} {:answer/id #uuid "f5af7b76-5c7d-52a9-8262-1e2bfb500412" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{7}⁡x}{7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547815} {:answer/id #uuid "fc69b155-6de6-5e6d-a369-6a5bbeeb4817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b7a4366-3be3-5b98-9a3e-ba4a656574ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547583} :answer-field/id #uuid "4338b54c-776e-5c97-9d8a-821ddad0e1e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d34958ec-5dd9-5a10-8bd6-7504a0d293c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-114951" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ using integration by parts." :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nI=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{8}⁡x & \\,⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{8{ln}^{7}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{8}⁡x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)(\\frac{8{ln}^{7}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{9}⁡x-8\\int \\frac{{ln}^{8}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{9}⁡x-8I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $I,$ remembering to add a constant of integration $C$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{9}⁡x-8I \\\\ 9I & ={ln}^{9}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{9}⁡x}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00674e18-ee02-59cd-b3dc-a6f698a1d859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547317} {:answer/id #uuid "89c053ae-f87b-53b5-b9ff-dddaf3e41c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547615} {:answer/id #uuid "8fea7db7-de80-5d17-aee5-ba303c413efc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547636} {:answer/id #uuid "910d3bad-81e3-5314-864f-7990eaa9b236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547640} {:answer/id #uuid "a5081cba-4b6f-5bf9-b175-aba9c3e125ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00674e18-ee02-59cd-b3dc-a6f698a1d859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547317} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d349dd1-b5a9-5935-ae21-26e3ea46f260" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f371caa1-75a3-5280-8f9d-dfa2d56f63d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-83246" :question/problem "A golden eagle is moving in a straight line with a velocity of $v(t)=\\frac{{2}^{t}}{12}\\,\\text{m/s},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find the average velocity of the golden eagle on the interval $[2,6].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the average value of the velocity on $[{t}_{1},{t}_{2}],$ we use the average value formula\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{6-2}\\,{\\int }_{2}^{6}(\\frac{{2}^{t}}{12})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 12\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{t}){|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{6}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{48\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average velocity is $\\frac{5}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b2745c4-1441-5995-aaf6-0adbc665aef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}=\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548213} {:answer/id #uuid "37d4082a-8911-5b7c-b50a-10c433cbf0c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2}=\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548215} {:answer/id #uuid "da3ff54f-5a25-50da-b204-8695bcb437f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548257} {:answer/id #uuid "ea42b5fa-8d2f-550e-9160-d2ffdc3c564b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}=\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548262} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1aadba-47b2-5657-83f5-47a149777663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37d4082a-8911-5b7c-b50a-10c433cbf0c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2}=\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548215} :answer-field/id #uuid "16be170c-c7ff-5a97-93c7-1025786aea44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d76f3ef-fa61-505b-b0e2-335ce30237ba" :question/problem "![](images/07/0760173ffa9ede7dc926ff42ddc2a2474433408c2320bc6e37f7dc95ffde2dd1.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(- \\frac{9}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and the point $X(-7,3)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(-7-(- \\frac{9}{2}))}^{2}+{(3-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(- \\frac{5}{2})^{2}+{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(- \\frac{9}{2}))^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2} & =(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2})^{2} \\\\ (x+\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{25}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "142cb5cf-eb17-5a66-bddf-259d1c11d2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{5}{2})^{2}=\\frac{49}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548205} {:answer/id #uuid "598a7dfc-10dc-5ab3-9d18-c3cf3f01773e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-\\frac{1}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{5}{2})^{2}=\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548226} {:answer/id #uuid "679c2f50-d4ed-5457-be54-4c6140240698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{5}{2})^{2}=\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548231} {:answer/id #uuid "74cfed18-39c2-5554-8a0a-adc370f4e424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-\\frac{1}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{5}{2})^{2}=\\frac{49}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548235} {:answer/id #uuid "c334d9c2-5306-529f-bcff-4b35925d958a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math