type/math} :answer/value "(x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{5}{2})^{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548247} {:answer/id #uuid "a8978ff3-c260-5405-8136-a305f8c97e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{5}{2})^{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548249} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b78d40-1db1-560a-8609-e5217894e2c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{5}{2})^{2}=\\frac{41}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84261f74-6d85-52e4-8990-c9b734242678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{5}{2})^{2}=\\frac{41}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548242} :answer-field/id #uuid "754128f9-ec01-5d20-b0df-c3585307d301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f346593-7a68-5095-ae90-07ccf78232e2" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2f1cdddf07f324c01491e359f347484f0feb69e471aaba93e1f410f7584df90.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(- \\frac{7}{2},- \\frac{5}{2})$ and the point $X(-8,-3)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(-8-(- \\frac{7}{2}))}^{2}+{(-3-(- \\frac{5}{2}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(- \\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(- \\frac{1}{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{41}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(- \\frac{7}{2}))^{2}+(y-(- \\frac{5}{2}))^{2} & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{2})^{2} \\\\ (x+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{5}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{41}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "075ae8f3-57cb-5b60-ba7d-7b522771c11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548370} {:answer/id #uuid "1b055083-25b0-50bd-915e-94df9cc030f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548400} {:answer/id #uuid "321c8f80-1da5-598f-8ac9-48d513b22952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548454} {:answer/id #uuid "a9848c7d-6940-59ef-bdc8-e27fe80c778e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548689} {:answer/id #uuid "b874085c-4187-5a6d-8924-748d9c4f64b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "075ae8f3-57cb-5b60-ba7d-7b522771c11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548370} :answer-field/id #uuid "810830be-38ed-57ee-8e7a-4d8bf93a0313" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0404d67e-b2ba-59db-a275-bcabad92e41d" :question/math-academy-id "q-145010" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{-} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{-}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))^{′}}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi ) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2\\cdot {0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, and the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (3\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2\\cdot {0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/f9/f9a4fc8fa61dac84d3dbc4bc48f86e2b702f7854b1fd1d3b3e5f4303fe7769a7.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e4d7f5-507c-5de7-88d4-ea15a0e80797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548360} {:answer/id #uuid "20d01cf0-d95f-5916-b4f3-763c5a6e9849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548416} {:answer/id #uuid "75b52c4a-dd39-5248-b7ac-60a9f8d0e11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548570} {:answer/id #uuid "7da7cab9-5764-5643-bc50-07cd0bd2f864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548591} {:answer/id #uuid "af09275f-fbfc-52f1-b658-e8836b59b4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7da7cab9-5764-5643-bc50-07cd0bd2f864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548591} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec36f9e6-099c-5944-9227-f178df8ff3ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0e768435-2535-570e-8108-8e3e9452ca28" :question/math-academy-id "q-145215" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2),\\,\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(0)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0$" :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & ={tan}^{2}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)({cos}^{2}⁡(0)+2) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1\\cdot ({1}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {0}^{+},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2)}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{{tan}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)({cos}^{2}⁡(0)+2)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 1\\cdot ({1}^{2}+2)}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the right limit of our derivative is not infinite, the curve does *not* have a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0.$ In fact, it is a horizontal tangent.\n\n![](images/49/498b937eba18870a36ee6f09bb99cf7dde38eb95c4cc2aa6edcb46080d0fcf04.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab31b463-8c69-583b-bac4-69800615c0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab31b463-8c69-583b-bac4-69800615c0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548692} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d0f1a43-0957-545b-b55f-b92a258588e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548886} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80554ead-73b4-5cfc-97e9-c1eb4541352d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80554ead-73b4-5cfc-97e9-c1eb4541352d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548599} :answer-field/id #uuid "95aff7d4-416c-5aea-a5fc-e652654519e7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "132df1ea-ba8a-54a4-a06a-964d1593a041" :question/math-academy-id "q-353034" :question/problem "Compute the values of $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi )$ for which the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ has a horizontal tangent, given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn **ascending order**, the polar curve has horizontal tangents at\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We have a horizontal tangent when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta ).$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta $ where ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ 2\\theta  & =\\pi n \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only two belong to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=0,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether any candidates have ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$\n\nWe substitute our candidate solutions into ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and check that ${x}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.$ If ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is also a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our curve has horizontal tangents at ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/b6/b60ac3e170875d48a6bf3733122b79be16ddff27c0cb3878d8a3125d09bb3824.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e072a8a-4c4e-573b-8869-941c8567a11b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=8(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548445} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7f9396-d71d-5308-95cc-a34867a20ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=8(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548562} {:answer/id #uuid "7a134ec0-3a05-56eb-ad2c-0e83ebd565ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=-8(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548582} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5d3114-e499-590c-baf7-861605384f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=4(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548587} {:answer/id #uuid "dc78eb36-9d33-50e7-9b6c-c918281f4ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=4(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e7f9396-d71d-5308-95cc-a34867a20ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=8(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548562} :answer-field/id #uuid "314ab3a0-11b2-5836-a9a6-779f4e2cfc86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13a87bfd-ec89-5321-9298-2cba42bc0bbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-106506" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+1=2{t}^{2},\\,y+3=4t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x+1}{2},\\,t=\\frac{y+3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y+3}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x+1}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =16\\cdot (\\frac{x+1}{2}) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =8(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9f12d8b-5eba-5053-9ae2-25dc56f3092e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9f12d8b-5eba-5053-9ae2-25dc56f3092e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548779} :answer-field/id #uuid "54a07fba-ccc2-5984-811e-36401527375b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548910} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067a4aa5-e856-53d1-a787-9e23be107370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "067a4aa5-e856-53d1-a787-9e23be107370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548367} :answer-field/id #uuid "89ff50ea-6ada-5e88-ad68-fd426c6d4ccb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1731e842-6496-5676-8984-724a57e1229e" :question/math-academy-id "q-277889" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x}>-(0.52)^{4x-3}.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<\\,x\\,<$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Since ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x}$ is always positive and $-(0.52)^{4x-3}$ is always negative, we always have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x}>-(0.52)^{4x-3}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the given inequality is satisfied by all real values of $x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-\\infty <x<\\infty \n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "223d1a4a-f12e-53dc-b2c2-3f53b62f9cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548423} {:answer/id #uuid "8b172897-6256-56d7-a919-3132661e3862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548622} {:answer/id #uuid "8b95b35a-939e-5d64-a7a0-741bd14bb687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548624} {:answer/id #uuid "8dbf5972-ecfe-5bfa-9e97-3f8d36df9174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548631} {:answer/id #uuid "99df87b4-dd35-52a1-a81f-94fcade66b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99df87b4-dd35-52a1-a81f-94fcade66b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548658} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a716f11-43d9-5db6-97b8-516caa9bdfb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1faddb6e-7103-543e-9a46-6187e7487263" :question/math-academy-id "q-109811" :question/problem "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{4}{3},\\,-\\frac{16}{3},\\,…$\n\nII. $-5,\\,-20,\\,80,\\,…$\n\nIII. $f(n+1)=4f(n)-4,\\,f(1)=2,\\,n\\ge 1$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{4}{3})}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{16}{3})}{(-\\frac{4}{3})}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{-20}{-5}=4 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{80}{-20}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not a geometric sequence. To see this, we can calculate the first $3$ terms of the sequence and then compute the ratios of consecutive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =2 \\\\ f(2) & =4\\cdot 2-4=4 \\\\ f(3) & =4\\cdot 4-4=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first two pairs of consecutive terms have different ratios:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)} & =\\frac{4}{2}=2 \\\\ \\frac{f(3)}{f(2)} & =\\frac{12}{4}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, none of the given is a geometric sequence."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee3cb3c2-5894-5f26-9c6f-ed238aac2f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee3cb3c2-5894-5f26-9c6f-ed238aac2f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548822} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7515820-f81c-5626-80c9-573137eac7e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "231ef465-da5d-5746-b60b-222091ac14fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-363452" :question/problem "Compute the value of $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ for which the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ has a vertical tangent, given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nThe polar curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We have a vertical tangent when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta $ where ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 2\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi n \\\\ \\theta  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only one belongs to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether our candidate has ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solution into ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and check that ${y}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.$ If ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}))-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ is a valid solution.\n\nSo, our curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.$\n\n![](images/8c/8c77297d85a8cd9a10ed219af49635fcf76412f80895a4af7a567f5ff9c49ce3.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548988 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46e8889c-2762-51f7-87f7-db53f364092e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46e8889c-2762-51f7-87f7-db53f364092e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548490} :answer-field/id #uuid "371a26c6-e616-55a4-808d-6c9373c57780" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29977420-5db9-54aa-a722-39b76e50bb47" :question/math-academy-id "q-353079" :question/problem "Compute the values of $\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi )$ for which the polar curve $r=2$ has a horizontal tangent, given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe polar curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =$ \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We have a horizontal tangent when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta $ where ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only one belongs to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether our candidate has ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solution into ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and check that ${x}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.$ If ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-2(-1) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, our curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/51/515b40ad43d2299d0e8eb0521acee460b232794f488b4a0a7325b61516157bff.png)"}]
